# ThreadSanitizer suppressions. Each entry names the finding it defers, the way
# the tool_check*.todo lists do: an entry is a backlog item, not a permission.
#
#   TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=<libxs>/.tsan.supp <executable>
#
# Note that a threaded run under ThreadSanitizer needs ASLR disabled on some
# kernels (`setarch -R`), or the runtime aborts with "unexpected memory mapping"
# before main() is reached.
#
# What is NOT in here, and cannot be: a race whose two accesses are the main
# thread and an OpenMP worker on opposite sides of a `omp parallel` region.
# GNU libgomp carries no ThreadSanitizer annotations and does not even link
# pthread (its barriers are raw futex calls), so the fork and join edges are
# invisible and every buffer handed into or out of a region is reported. Those
# stacks run through the code under test, so suppressing them would hide real
# findings alongside. Build with an instrumented runtime instead (clang plus a
# libomp built with TSan support, which ships libarcher.so) to get a signal that
# covers the region boundaries.

# libxs_barrier_wait spins on LIBXS_SYNC_CYCLE, which dereferences the epoch
# plainly while the last arrival increments it atomically. The wait can only end
# through the atomic load that guards the cycle, so the release edge is atomic
# either way and the plain read costs the spin nothing.
race:libxs_barrier_wait
